==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɹoʊl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-prole.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊl|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{m|en|proletariat||working class}} by shortening.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} A member of the [[proletariat]]; a [[proletarian]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[pleb]] {{gloss|ordinary person}}.

===Etymology 2===
From {{m|en|prowl}} obsolete pronunciation (/pɹoʊl/), from {{inh|en|enm|prollen}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[prowl#Verb|prowl]]; to [[proll]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Fitzherbert|title=A Treatise Concerning Policy and Religion|chapter=VIII|publisher=|year=1652|page=57|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_a-treatise-concerning-po_fitzherbert-thomas_1652/page/56/mode/2up?q=%22to+prole%22|text= {{...}} for he ordained, {{...}} and that boyes and children ſhould have ſo little allowed them to eat, that they ſhould be forced to '''prole''', and ſteal for their better proviſion, to make them thereby the more induſtrious, nimble, and quick of ſpirit, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Sadler|title=Poems on various subjects. To which is added, The merry miller: or, The country-man's ramble to London, a farce|year=1766|chapter=The Peddlar. A Tale.|page=116|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_poems-on-various-subject_sadler-thomas_1766/page/n129/mode/1up?q=prole&view=theater|text=But I will not ſuch Journies take, / To dig and '''prole''' in vain: / For was I to dig twenty Weeks, / Without might come again.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mary Elizabeth Braddon|title=To the Bitter End|chapter="Some Innocents 'Scape Not The Thunderbol."|chapter_number=XLIV|publisher=John Maxwell and Co.|year=1873|page=351|pageurl=https://www.google.com.sv/books/edition/To_the_Bitter_End/vYrrZHrV0Y0C?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=%22proling%22&pg=PA351&printsec=frontcover|text={{...}} and Joseph went '''proling''' about after dark with his gun—and took and shot him!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:George Carew (diplomat)|George Carew]]|editor=Ernest George Atkinson|title=Calendar of the State Papers relating to Ireland, of the reigns of Henry VIII, Edward VI., Mary, and Elizabeth. 1600 March-October|section=108|year=1903|page=391|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/1903calendarofstatep09greauoft/page/390/mode/2up?q=%22to+prole%22|text="This summer, by reason of continual employment abroad in service, not having leisure to '''prole''' for hawks, I cannot make that present unto you as I desired. {{...}}”}}

